\section{Proofs}
\label{app:proofs}

\paragraph{Proof of \cref{prop:floor}.}
Condition on the $M$ records. The labeled set is a simple random subset of size $n$, so $\bar h_{\mathrm{lab}}-\bar h_{\mathrm{all}}$ has conditional mean zero and conditional variance $(\frac1n-\frac1M)S_h^2$, where $S_h^2=\frac1{M-1}\sum_i(h_i-\bar h_{\mathrm{all}})^2$. By \eqref{eq:est}, $\hat\theta_g=\bar Y_{\mathrm{all}}+(\bar h_{\mathrm{lab}}-\bar h_{\mathrm{all}})$; the first term is a function of the records and the second has conditional mean zero, so they are uncorrelated and $\Var\hat\theta_g=\Var(Y)/M+(\frac1n-\frac1M)\E S_h^2$. For i.i.d.\ records $\E S_h^2=\Var(h)$, which gives \eqref{eq:Vg}. Writing $Y-g=(Y-m)+(m-g)$, whose terms are uncorrelated, $\Var(Y-g)=\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}+\Var(m-g)$, and $\Var(Y)=\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}+V_m$. Substituting gives \eqref{eq:floor}. \hfill$\square$

\paragraph{PPI++ as the best linear member.}
For $g=\lambda f$ the estimator is $\lambda\bar f_{\mathrm{unl}}+\bar{(Y-\lambda f)}_{\mathrm{lab}}$, with variance $\Var(Y-\lambda f)/n+\lambda^2\Var(f)/N$ because the two parts use disjoint records. Setting the derivative in $\lambda$ to zero gives $\lambda^\star$ and \eqref{eq:ppipp}. For the pooled estimator with $g=bf$, $\frac1M(n\bar f_{\mathrm{lab}}+N\bar f_{\mathrm{unl}})b+\bar Y_{\mathrm{lab}}-b\bar f_{\mathrm{lab}}=\frac{bN}{M}\bar f_{\mathrm{unl}}+\bar{(Y-\tfrac{bN}M f)}_{\mathrm{lab}}$, i.e.\ PPI with $\lambda=bN/M$. With the best linear $g$, $\Var(Y-g)=\Var(Y)(1-\rho^2)$ in \eqref{eq:Vg} gives $\frac{\Var(Y)}n[\frac nM+(1-\frac nM)(1-\rho^2)]=\frac{\Var(Y)}n(1-\rho^2\frac NM)$, equal to \eqref{eq:ppipp}. \hfill$\square$

\paragraph{The clip.}
By \eqref{eq:floor} with $g=a+cf$ for any constant $a$, $V(g)-\Vstar=(\frac1n-\frac1M)\Var(m-a-cf)$. The projection of $m(f)$ on $(1,f)$ has slope $b^\star$ and residual $m-a^\star-b^\star f$, uncorrelated with $f$ with variance $\Delta$; hence $\Var(m-a-cf)=\Delta+(b^\star-c)^2\Var(f)$, which is \eqref{eq:clip}. With $c=b^\star$ this is \eqref{eq:gap}. \hfill$\square$

\emph{Check on synthetic populations.} We drew $f\sim N(0,1)$ and $Y=b f+\sigma\varepsilon$ (so $\Delta=0$) for $b\in\{-0.5,0,0.8,1,1.2,1.5,2\}$ and $\sigma=0.6$ at $n\in\{30,100,300,10^3,3\cdot10^3\}$ with $N=10^4$, at $n=300$ with $N\in\{300,1200\}$, and for $\sigma\in\{0.3,1.5\}$ at $n\in\{100,10^3\}$ ($61$ cells, $4000$ to $8000$ repetitions). The paired width ratio of the unclipped to the package's PPI++ differs from $\sqrt{V(\lambda^\star)/V(\mathrm{clip}\,\lambda^\star)}$ by at most $0.004$ at $n\ge10^3$, $0.014$ at $n=300$, $0.088$ at $n=100$ and $0.131$ at $n=30$; the difference is the cost of estimating $\lambda$. For $0\le b\le1$ the ratio is within $0.013$ of one at $n\ge100$, and at $n=30$ the unclipped interval is up to $4.6\%$ wider (the clip then shrinks a noisy $\hat\lambda$). For $b<0$ the package sets $\lambda=0$ and returns the classical interval, and the unclipped interval is $0.77$ to $0.89$ as wide.
\Cref{fig:clip} shows the sweep with the real datasets at their reference $n$, and \cref{tab:decomp} splits the width ratio on four datasets into the spread of the estimate and the ratio of its standard error to that spread, arm by arm on the same draws.

