\section{Closed-form excess and the headroom diagnostic}
\label{app:diagnostic}\label{app:propcheck}

\Cref{fig:propcheck} plots, for every synthetic population and every real dataset with a closed form, the measured excess width of each estimator over the estimated floor against the prediction of \eqref{eq:gap}, plus the learning cost of the calibrator where the estimator is cross-fitted: $(\tfrac1n-\tfrac1M)[\Delta+(\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}+\Delta)\,2/n_{\mathrm{train}}]$ for the pooled linear estimator and $(\tfrac1n-\tfrac1M)\,7\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}/n_{\mathrm{train}}$ for the spline, with $n_{\mathrm{train}}=4n/5$.

The diagnostic uses only the labeled records. From the cross-fitted residual variances $v_{\mathrm{lin}}$ and $v_{\mathrm{spl}}$ of the linear and the spline calibrators it forms $\hat\sigma^2=v_{\mathrm{spl}}/(1+7/n_{\mathrm{train}})$, $\hat\Delta=v_{\mathrm{lin}}/(1+2/n_{\mathrm{train}})-\hat\sigma^2$ and the learning cost $7\hat\sigma^2/n_{\mathrm{train}}$; its predicted relative width gain is $1-\sqrt{1-(\tfrac1n-\tfrac1M)(\hat\Delta-\mathrm{cost})/\mathrm{SE}^2_{\mathrm{PPI}{+}{+}}}$, with medians over repetitions. \Cref{fig:diagnostic} compares it with the realised gain, $1-r$ for the spline-calibrated arm.

