\section{Finite-population check}
\label{app:finitepop}

On \texttt{galaxies} ($16{,}743$ records), \texttt{gene\_expression} ($61{,}150$) and \texttt{census\_income} ($380{,}091$) we redrew the labeled and unlabeled records disjointly and without replacement, with $N=10^4$, and compared the SD of the PPI++ estimate over repetitions ($2000$ for $n\ge300$, so about $\pm0.02$) with the i.i.d.\ cell of the same $n$. With $P$ records, the approximation $\sqrt{[\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}(1-n/P)/n+V_g(1-M/P)/M]/[\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}/n+V_g/M]}$ gives a predicted ratio. The observed ratio falls with $M/P$ as predicted (\texttt{galaxies}, $n=3000$: $0.828$ against $0.831$; below one in every \texttt{galaxies} cell with $n\ge300$), with differences from the approximation of up to $0.04$, the size of the scatter on \texttt{census\_income}, where no effect is predicted. On \texttt{gene\_expression} at $n=500$ and $1000$ the observed ratio lies above the approximation ($0.994$ against $0.964$; $0.987$ against $0.946$), so the approximation is not quantitative there. The interval assumes i.i.d.\ draws, so it over-covers where the SD falls (\texttt{galaxies}, $n=3000$: $0.98$ against $0.95$): a finite-pool effect, not a failure of the arm. All other real-data cells draw i.i.d.\ with replacement from the pool (\texttt{ballots}: from its labeled records), as in \cref{sec:experiments}.

\section{Compute and reproducibility}
\label{app:repro}

\paragraph{Environment and data.} Python 3.11.2 on CPU only, with \texttt{numpy} 2.4.6, \texttt{scipy} 1.17.1, \texttt{pandas} 3.0.6, \texttt{scikit-learn} 1.9.1 and \texttt{ppi-python} 0.2.3 (the PPI and PPI++ baselines). The data are the nine example sets of \texttt{ppi-python}, fetched by its loader; no model is trained, and the supplied predictions are the fixed predictor. SHA-256 sums and shapes of the files used are recorded with the results. The literature search for prior work (seven queries on named related work) did not meet the paper that the task excludes.

\paragraph{Randomness.} Each cell (population, $n$, $N$) has the seed $20261126+\mathrm{crc32}(\text{cell id}) \bmod 10^6$ and repetition $r$ uses \texttt{SeedSequence}$([\text{seed},r])$, so a cell does not depend on the number of processes. Intervals for ratios use a paired bootstrap over repetitions ($300$ resamples) with fixed seeds.

\paragraph{Compute.} The experiments took about $13.4$ CPU-hours: $12.8$ recorded per cell (the sum of wall-clock seconds, each cell on one CPU of a shared host, so an upper bound) and $0.6$ for the clip sweep ($0.19$) and the unclipped re-runs (about $0.45$, estimated, not timed per job). The recorded part splits as $1.6$ for the synthetic experiments, $5.6$ for the real-data grid, $1.3$ for interval constructions, $2.8$ for side information, $1.5$ for the failure regimes and $0.06$ for the $N/n$ slice; the longest cell took $22$ minutes.

\paragraph{From the code to the exhibits.} \Cref{tab:scripts} maps each exhibit to the script that draws it. Every script reads the result files built by \texttt{analysis/build\_results.py} from the stored cells, and a missing or inconsistent input stops it. The commands that run each experiment are in the README of the code directory.

